{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791234663,"nonce":"czEY5oc2V86kN65FXMridsilisc=","title":"Stdlib is not found when rayzor runs through a symlink on Linux","message":"`discover_stdlib_paths` walks up from `std::env::current_exe()` looking for haxe-std. On Linux that is `/proc/self/exe`, which resolves symlinks; on macOS it is the invoked path. A binary symlinked out of its checkout (a `ln -s` into /usr/local/bin, or the nuc gate's `target/release/rayzor -\u003e ~/rz-bisect-target/release/rayzor`) therefore searches from the real file's directory, finds no haxe-std, and compiles with only the built-in types. Nothing is printed: the `warn!` goes nowhere.\n\nThe Linux jemalloc re-exec in src/main.rs also restarts through `current_exe()`, so argv[0] is replaced by the resolved path before discovery ever runs.\n\nSymptoms: `Cannot find type 'haxe.Exception'` / `'Math'` / `'Reflect'`, `uncompiled Map.new`, `uncompiled StringBuf.new`, `Cannot access field 'length'`.\n\nMeasured on the nuc: conformance 690 PASS vs 837 on the Mac; 149 rows pass on the Mac and fail on Linux. Five of them (Issue10098, Issue10018, Issue11466, TestNaN, Issue10304) all pass with RAYZOR_STD_PATH pointing at compiler/haxe-std.\n\nFix: also walk up from argv[0] when it has a directory part, and keep argv[0] across the jemalloc re-exec (`CommandExt::arg0`).","files":null}]}